= Halo effect — Simple =

<onlyinclude>The '''halo effect''' is a bias in judgment in which a favorable or unfavorable overall impression of a person, object or organization influences ratings of more specific qualities. For example, a supervisor who thinks highly of an employee may also give unusually high ratings for unrelated skills without enough evidence about those skills. The idea has been studied in psychological ratings since Edward Thorndike's 1920 research.<ref name="thorndike">Edward L. Thorndike, [https://doi.org/10.1037/h0071663 “A Constant Error in Psychological Ratings”], ''Journal of Applied Psychology'' 4, 25–29 (1920), original research on correlated ratings.</ref>

== An everyday example ==

Suppose a teacher is especially friendly and engaging. Students may infer that the teacher is also more knowledgeable or fair, even if the students have not independently evaluated those other qualities.

The important issue is not whether the teacher really is knowledgeable. It is whether a judgment about one quality spills into another judgment without enough independent evidence.

== Can a bad first impression create an effect? ==

Yes. Discussions sometimes call the negative counterpart a “horn effect.” A general unfavorable impression can contaminate judgments about specific skills or actions. The expression describes a possible bias, not a certainty about every evaluator.

== When are correlated ratings reasonable? ==

Some real traits are genuinely related. A competent and conscientious worker may perform well across several tasks. If ratings correlate, this could reflect real associations, a shared evaluator bias or both.

Therefore, one cannot prove a halo effect simply by observing that all of a person's ratings are high.

== How can researchers study it? ==

Researchers may hold relevant performance information constant while experimentally changing an unrelated positive or negative impression, then examine whether ratings of other qualities shift. Independent raters, blinding and objective measures help distinguish bias from genuine trait differences.

Richard Nisbett and Timothy Wilson investigated how global evaluations can affect specific judgments and people's accounts of why they made those judgments.<ref name="nisbett">Richard E. Nisbett and Timothy D. Wilson, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.35.4.250 “The Halo Effect: Evidence for Unconscious Alteration of Judgments”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 35, 250–256 (1977).</ref>

== Where might it matter? ==

Halo effects can influence hiring, school assessment, product reviews, clinical impressions and public discussion. The direction and size of an effect depend on the task, evidence and evaluator.

A review process can reduce some risks by separating criteria, collecting evidence before overall impressions and using independent assessments. Those interventions must be evaluated rather than presumed perfect.

== What it does not mean ==

The halo effect does not establish that attractive people are always evaluated more favorably, that all positive judgments are errors or that a given individual is biased. Such claims would require task-specific evidence.

The topic connects to [[Cognition]], [[Reasoning]], [[Metacognition]] and studies of decisions and measurement.

